B.Ob=new A.ic(B.lK,"The 1st House begins the chart at the Ascendant, the exact point that was rising on the eastern horizon at the moment of birth. It represents the self as it meets the world \u2014 identity in motion, not identity in private. Where the Sun sign describes core purpose and the Moon describes inner emotional life, the 1st House describes the outward approach: how a person walks into a room, and the impression that forms before a single word is spoken.","Physical appearance and vitality, personal identity, first impressions, self-image, temperament, and the general approach a person takes toward new situations and new people.",'Beginnings, initiative, self-assertion, and the mask worn (not insincerely, but adaptively) before deeper layers of personality are shown. This is the house of "the self as project," constantly being built and revised through action.',"A planet in the 1st House colors the personality strongly and often behaves almost like a second Sun sign \u2014 its themes become part of how a person is perceived on sight. A stellium here can make someone feel unmistakably vivid or larger-than-life in ordinary interaction.",'A transit through the 1st House tends to affect the body, self-image, and general life direction directly and personally \u2014 this is often felt as "something about me is changing" rather than "something external is changing."',"The sign on the 1st House cusp is the Ascendant/Rising sign itself, arguably the most personally worn sign in the whole chart alongside Sun and Moon \u2014 see My Big Three for how it interacts with those two.")
B.Oa=new A.ic(B.lL,'The 2nd House follows the Ascendant and turns attention from "who am I" to "what is mine." It governs personal resources in the broadest sense: money and possessions, yes, but also innate talents, self-worth, and the values that quietly decide what a person considers worth having at all.','Income, personal property, material security, natural talents and skills, self-esteem, and the personal value system that shapes spending, saving, and what one considers "enough."',"Stability, self-sufficiency, and the slow accumulation of both resources and confidence. This house rewards patience over haste \u2014 its themes build gradually rather than arriving suddenly.","A planet here often shows where a person finds their most reliable sense of security, and frequently points to a genuine talent or income-generating skill tied to that planet's nature.","Transits through the 2nd House commonly coincide with shifts in income, spending habits, or a reevaluation of what actually matters enough to spend money, time, or effort protecting.","The sign here shows the *style* of earning and valuing \u2014 a fixed sign here suggests steady, durable resource-building; a mutable sign suggests more varied or adaptable income sources.")
B.Oe=new A.ic(B.lO,"The 3rd House governs the immediate, everyday mind: how a person talks, learns, and moves through their local world. It is the house of the short trip rather than the long journey, the quick message rather than the philosophical treatise \u2014 that broader reach belongs to the opposite 9th House.","Communication style, early education, siblings and cousins, short trips and daily commuting, curiosity, and the neighborhood or immediate community.","Information exchange, mental agility, and the constant small connections \u2014 conversations, errands, messages \u2014 that make up ordinary daily life.","A planet here often makes someone a natural communicator or perpetual learner in that planet's flavor \u2014 restless curiosity for quicker planets, deeper or slower-forming interests for the outer ones.","Transits through the 3rd House often coincide with an uptick in messages, short trips, sibling contact, or simply a busier, more talkative stretch of daily life.","The cusp sign flavors how someone naturally communicates day to day \u2014 airy signs here tend toward quick, idea-driven exchange; earthy signs toward practical, matter-of-fact conversation.")
B.Of=new A.ic(B.lP,"The 4th House sits at the Imum Coeli, the chart's lowest and most private point. It is the house of roots: home, family, ancestry, and the emotional foundation a person returns to beneath every public role they play. Where the 10th House (its opposite) is the public peak, the 4th is the private base.","Home and household, family of origin, ancestry and heritage, emotional foundations, and \u2014 traditionally \u2014 one parent (conventions differ on which; modern astrology often reads it as the more nurturing or domestically present parent).",'Belonging, privacy, memory, and the felt sense of "home" wherever that ends up being built, whether that matches the family of origin or is consciously built differently from it.',"A planet here often shows what a person most needs to feel secure at home, and can describe an early domestic atmosphere that continues shaping adult needs around privacy and belonging.","Transits through the 4th House commonly coincide with home moves, family developments, or a pull inward toward rest and reflection after a more outward-facing chapter.",'The IC sign colors the emotional flavor of "home" \u2014 a water sign here suggests home as emotional sanctuary; an air sign suggests home as a place of conversation and mental ease as much as comfort.')
B.Og=new A.ic(B.lQ,"The 5th House is the house of joyful, self-expressive creation: romance in its playful, courting phase, creative work made for its own sake, and the particular delight of being genuinely enjoyed by life rather than merely managing it.","Romance and dating, creative and artistic expression, hobbies, play, children, and risk-taking done for pleasure rather than necessity.","Self-expression, generosity of spirit, performance (in the broad sense of showing one's work to the world), and the willingness to be seen having fun.","A planet here often points to where someone shines when given permission to simply enjoy themselves, and frequently describes a genuine creative gift in that planet's style.","Transits through the 5th House often coincide with a new romance, a burst of creative output, or simply a season that feels more playful and less duty-bound than usual.","The cusp sign shapes the *style* of self-expression \u2014 a fire sign here suggests bold, visible creativity; a water sign suggests creativity as emotional processing, more private in its origin even if publicly shared.")
B.Oi=new A.ic(B.lR,"The 6th House governs the unglamorous but load-bearing parts of life: daily work, routine, health habits, and service rendered to others without much fanfare. It asks what a person actually does with a Tuesday, not what they aspire to on their best day.","Daily work and employment, physical health and wellness routines, habits, service to others, and the small disciplines that keep ordinary life functioning.","Competence, maintenance, and the quiet dignity of tasks done well and consistently, even when no one is watching or applauding.","A planet here often shows where a person finds meaning through usefulness, and can point to both a work style and a health sensitivity connected to that planet's nature.","Transits through the 6th House commonly coincide with changes to daily routine, workload, or health habits \u2014 a season for reorganizing how the ordinary days actually run.","The cusp sign shapes someone's natural approach to work and routine \u2014 a mutable sign here often means a flexible, varied daily rhythm; a fixed sign, a strong preference for consistent habits.")
B.Od=new A.ic(B.lS,'The 7th House sits opposite the Ascendant at the Descendant, the point of "the other." It governs one-on-one partnership of every committed kind: marriage and long-term romance, but also business partners and, notably, open rivals \u2014 anyone met as an equal counterpart rather than as a group.',"Marriage and committed partnership, business partnerships, contracts, and \u2014 traditionally \u2014 open enemies or rivals, the people one faces directly rather than a faceless crowd.","Balance, compromise, mirroring, and the parts of the self that often only become visible through a significant other's reflection.","A planet here often describes what someone seeks in a partner, and can also describe qualities projected onto partners before being consciously owned as one's own.","Transits through the 7th House commonly coincide with relationship developments \u2014 new partnerships forming, existing ones being renegotiated, or contracts and agreements coming to the fore.","The Descendant sign describes the *type* of partner and partnership style someone is drawn to \u2014 often complementary to the Ascendant sign's own qualities, since the two points sit exactly opposite.")
B.O8=new A.ic(B.lT,"The 8th House governs what is shared, merged, or transformed: intimacy, other people's resources (inheritance, debt, shared finances), and the psychological undercurrents \u2014 including loss and regeneration \u2014 that don't stay on the surface of life.","Intimacy and sexuality, shared finances and joint resources, debt and inheritance, and deep psychological transformation, including how a person meets loss, crisis, and rebirth.","Depth, vulnerability, trust, and the willingness to be changed by an experience rather than merely passing through it.","A planet here often points to where a person experiences the most profound transformation over a lifetime, and can describe both a gift for depth and a tendency toward secrecy around that planet's themes.","Transits through the 8th House often coincide with intense, below-the-surface periods \u2014 shared-resource matters, intimacy shifts, or a genuine psychological turning point.","The cusp sign shapes how someone handles intimacy and crisis \u2014 a cardinal sign here tends to confront transformation head-on; a fixed sign tends to resist it until change becomes unavoidable, then holds the result tightly.")
B.O7=new A.ic(B.lU,"The 9th House is the house of the long horizon: higher education, philosophy, foreign travel and cultures, and the belief systems (religious, ethical, or simply personal) that give life a larger sense of meaning beyond the everyday facts covered by the 3rd House.","Higher education, philosophy and belief systems, long-distance travel, foreign cultures and languages, publishing, and the search for meaning.","Expansion, exploration, and the confidence that comes from testing one's worldview against something genuinely unfamiliar.","A planet here often shows where someone seeks meaning and growth, and can describe a real gift for teaching, travel, or philosophical thinking in that planet's style.","Transits through the 9th House commonly coincide with study, travel opportunities, or a genuine shift in worldview or belief.","The cusp sign colors how someone pursues meaning \u2014 a fire sign here suggests an adventurous, experience-first approach to belief and travel; an earth sign, a more grounded, credential-and-evidence-minded one.")
B.O9=new A.ic(B.lV,"The 10th House sits at the Midheaven, the chart's highest and most public point. It governs vocation, reputation, and the long-term legacy a person builds in full view of the world \u2014 the direct opposite of the 4th House's private foundation.","Career and vocation, public reputation, long-term ambition, and \u2014 traditionally \u2014 the other parent (whichever was more associated with authority or public standing).","Achievement, visibility, responsibility, and the way private effort eventually becomes a public record.","A planet here often becomes closely tied to how a person is known professionally and publicly \u2014 it frequently shows up in career themes and reputation more visibly than almost any other placement.","Transits through the 10th House commonly coincide with career developments, public recognition (wanted or not), or a shift in life direction and ambition.","The Midheaven sign describes the *style* of public role someone is drawn toward \u2014 see the Natal Chart screen, where Midheaven sign is shown alongside the Ascendant.")
B.Oh=new A.ic(B.lM,"The 11th House governs the wider social world beyond one-on-one partnership: friendships, communities, group causes, and the hopes and long-range goals a person holds for the future, often shared with others of like mind.","Friendships and social circles, group memberships and communities, humanitarian causes, and long-term hopes, wishes, and future goals.","Belonging on a larger scale than romance or family, collective vision, and the specific kind of support that comes from people who chose each other rather than being related by blood.","A planet here often shows what someone brings to (and seeks from) friend groups and communities, and can describe a real talent for organizing or inspiring collective effort.","Transits through the 11th House often coincide with shifts in social circle, new group involvements, or renewed focus on future goals and the people who share them.","The cusp sign shapes the *type* of community someone gravitates toward \u2014 an air sign here favors idea-driven or intellectually stimulating groups; an earth sign favors practical, goal-oriented ones.")
B.Oc=new A.ic(B.lN,"The 12th House closes the wheel and governs what lies beneath ordinary waking awareness: the unconscious, solitude, hidden strengths and struggles, spiritual life, and endings that quietly prepare the way for the new beginning at the 1st House.","The unconscious mind, solitude and retreat, spirituality and inner life, hidden strengths or difficulties, and \u2014 traditionally \u2014 institutions of withdrawal from ordinary life.","Surrender, inner work, compassion, and the parts of a person that operate quietly in the background of a whole life, shaping it without always being consciously named.","A planet here often operates in a more private, internal, or gradually-realized way than the same planet placed more visibly elsewhere \u2014 its themes tend to be lived inwardly before (if ever) being shown outwardly.","Transits through the 12th House often coincide with rest, retreat, or quiet internal processing \u2014 a natural season for closing chapters before something new begins.","The cusp sign colors how someone experiences solitude and inner life \u2014 a social sign here can make solitude feel more effortful to claim; a naturally introspective sign can make the 12th House feel like genuine relief.")
